<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles de Sales</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1666">1666</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles de Sales was a French Knight Hospitaller who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a> as governor of the French part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and the order's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands in 1660. He was killed in 1666 fighting the English on Saint Kitts, shortly after the order had sold its rights to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles de Sales was born in 1625 at the Château de Sales, in what is today Thorens-Glières in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-savoie">Haute-Savoie</a>. His father, Louis, comte de Sales, was a noble of the Duchy of Savoy and a brother of Saint Francis de Sales. Charles became a chevalier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Order of Saint John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1643, fought in several of the order's campaigns against the Turks, and took part in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 1650.</p><p>In 1651 the bankrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-des-iles-de-lamerique">Compagnie des Îles de l'Amérique</a> sold the French part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Christopher Island</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-barthelemy">Saint Barthélemy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-martin-island">Saint Martin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a>, to the order for 120,000 écus, at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a> and with the agreement of the grand master Jean-Paul Lascaris-Castellar. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. In 1657, on the death of the Chevalier Charles de Montmagny, the order sent de Sales to govern alongside Poincy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1659 de Sales and Governor Charles Houël du Petit Pré of Guadeloupe signed for the French an offensive and defensive league with the English governors Roger Osborne of Montserrat and James Russell of Nevis against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Island Caribs</a> of the Leeward Islands. When Poincy died in 1660 the order, which had still not paid the whole purchase price, made de Sales governor, and he took office on 11 April 1660. Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> the Hospitallers gave up the islands in 1665, and in February 1665 the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> confirmed de Sales in his post.</p><p>As war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> grew likely, de Sales agreed with William Watts, governor of the English part of the island, in January 1666 that each side would warn the other when it heard war had been declared and wait three days before attacking. A mission he sent to the English governor general Francis Willoughby in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> received only evasive answers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fighting on Saint Christopher began on 21 April 1666, before the declaration of war reached the island, and two days later the French routed the larger English force. De Sales was killed on 22 April, and Watts also died in the fighting. Claude de Roux de Saint-Laurent then became governor of the whole island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles de Sales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Poincy's death in 1660, <b>Charles de Sales</b> succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/state/hospitaller-caribbean-colonies">Hospitaller Caribbean colonies</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thorens-Glières, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,6.25&amp;year=1656" aria-label="Thorens-Glières on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4698 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles de Sales"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5148 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5148 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,6.25&amp;year=1656">What was here in 1656? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
